<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Qi</h1><p class="st-meta">479 CE – 502 CE · capital Jiankang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second of the Southern Dynasties, short-lived and bloody, but remembered for the literary salon of Prince Jingling and the Yongming style of poetry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-daocheng">Xiao Daocheng</a>, a general who had become the power behind the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liu-song">Liu Song</a> throne, forced its last emperor to abdicate in 479 and founded the Qi dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>. Historians call it Southern Qi to distinguish it from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> (483 to 493) the south enjoyed relative peace. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-ziliang">Xiao Ziliang</a>, Prince of Jingling, gathered scholars and Buddhists at his residence, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-friends-of-jingling">Eight Friends of Jingling</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-yue">Shen Yue</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Xiao Yan</a>, who developed the tonal rules of Yongming-style verse. Later rulers, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ming-of-southern-qi">Emperor Ming</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-baojuan">Xiao Baojuan</a>, killed ministers and relatives in large numbers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Xiao Yan</a>, a distant relative and governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a>, rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-baojuan">Xiao Baojuan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and in 502 founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-qi.jpg" alt="Southern Qi" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Qi_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>479 CE – 502 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiankang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emperor Gao of Southern Qi, Emperor Wu of Southern Qi, Xiao Baojuan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qi (Xiao clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=490&amp;focus=southern-qi" aria-label="Southern Qi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Qi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8057 1267L8057 1267M8057 1267L8057 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8057 1267h0M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jiankang. <a href="/atlas?year=490&amp;focus=southern-qi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
